[2026-03-15T08:52:35.274Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TCcXB7WcyGocqXJSKj2mCf","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording application sessions, uploading them, and managing recordings for performance analysis and debugging.\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nCreate an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and set it as the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `record [url]` | Launch Replay Browser and record a session |\n| `upload [ids...]` | Upload recordings to Replay |\n| `list` | View all local recordings |\n| `login` / `logout` | Manage account access |\n| `remove [ids...]` | Delete recordings |\n| `info` | Show CLI and browser version info |\n| `update` | Update Replay Browser |\n\n## Quick Workflow\n\n1. **Record a session**: `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. **Upload when done**: `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. **View recording**: Returns shareable URL after upload\n\nUse `-h` or `--help` with any command for detailed options. For debugging recorded applications, install the Replay MCP Server in Claude using the `claude --mcp-config` command with your `REPLAY_API_KEY`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4b0b3d3-2c6a-4406-854f-49cde2c25320","uuid":"6e54a059-e211-4264-99e9-cbec9721c380","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a command-line tool for recording application sessions, uploading them, and managing recordings for performance analysis and debugging.\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nCreate an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and set it as the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| `record [url]` | Launch Replay Browser and record a session |\n| `upload [ids...]` | Upload recordings to Replay |\n| `list` | View all local recordings |\n| `login` / `logout` | Manage account access |\n| `remove [ids...]` | Delete recordings |\n| `info` | Show CLI and browser version info |\n| `update` | Update Replay Browser |\n\n## Quick Workflow\n\n1. **Record a session**: `replayio record https://myapp.com`\n2. **Upload when done**: `replayio upload <recording-id>`\n3. **View recording**: Returns shareable URL after upload\n\nUse `-h` or `--help` with any command for detailed options. For debugging recorded applications, install the Replay MCP Server in Claude using the `claude --mcp-config` command with your `REPLAY_API_KEY`.","durationMs":3387,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-15T08:52:36.693Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AqspUgomSRSt5sANrjnHmM","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Documentation Summary\n\n**Overview:**\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture browser activity comprehensively. It functions as an investigative platform where users can explore application behavior using a systematic approach: formulate questions, discover relevant events, understand causation, develop hypotheses, and support conclusions with gathered evidence.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\nThe recording operates as a database with two fundamental units:\n- **Points**: Discrete execution moments when JS statements run or significant events occur\n- **Elements**: DOM element references independent of specific points, existing throughout their lifetime\n\n**Available Tool Categories:**\n\n*Exploratory tools* help discover what occurred and when:\n- Error detection (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, storage access, network requests, React renders)\n- Source code investigation (file discovery, code reading, pattern searching with hit counts)\n\n*Explanatory tools* clarify causation:\n- Dependency tracking showing happens-before relationships\n- Stack frame analysis\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element inspection\n- Component tree visualization\n- Expression evaluation through logpoints and evaluations\n\n**Best Practices:**\nThe documentation recommends locating relevant code sections, verifying execution, examining variable states using logpoint tools for unexpected values, and employing ControlDependency analysis when investigating timing issues around specific events."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4b0b3d3-2c6a-4406-854f-49cde2c25320","uuid":"1bdd66e9-c207-4c10-9044-609e46b4bc68","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Documentation Summary\n\n**Overview:**\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture browser activity comprehensively. It functions as an investigative platform where users can explore application behavior using a systematic approach: formulate questions, discover relevant events, understand causation, develop hypotheses, and support conclusions with gathered evidence.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\nThe recording operates as a database with two fundamental units:\n- **Points**: Discrete execution moments when JS statements run or significant events occur\n- **Elements**: DOM element references independent of specific points, existing throughout their lifetime\n\n**Available Tool Categories:**\n\n*Exploratory tools* help discover what occurred and when:\n- Error detection (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, storage access, network requests, React renders)\n- Source code investigation (file discovery, code reading, pattern searching with hit counts)\n\n*Explanatory tools* clarify causation:\n- Dependency tracking showing happens-before relationships\n- Stack frame analysis\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element inspection\n- Component tree visualization\n- Expression evaluation through logpoints and evaluations\n\n**Best Practices:**\nThe documentation recommends locating relevant code sections, verifying execution, examining variable states using logpoint tools for unexpected values, and employing ControlDependency analysis when investigating timing issues around specific events.","durationMs":3957,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
